{
  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "lem:rational-grid-primal-error",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "Let \\(U_n\\) be the optimum returned by the rational action-grid program in\n\\(\\mathrm{def:evaluator\\text{-}handle}\\), with \\(G=\\lceil n^{3/4}\\rceil\\),\nunder the fixed independent allocation \\(q^\\dagger\\).  Then\n\\[\nR_n(c^\\dagger)\\le U_n\n\\le \\frac1n-\\Lambda_{c^\\dagger}n^{-4/3}\n+B_{c^\\dagger}n^{-3/2}+\\frac1{4G^2}.\n\\]\nConsequently, the diagonal evaluator's rational primal error may be chosen to\nsatisfy\n\\[\ne_{\\mathrm{pr},n}\n\\le (B_{c^\\dagger}+1/4)n^{-1/6}+2^{-n},\n\\]\nand hence \\(e_{\\mathrm{pr},n}\\to0\\).  This is ordinary convergence; the\ndisplay does not furnish a computable all-future modulus because\n\\(B_{c^\\dagger}\\) is not supplied as computable data.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "n",
      "c^\\dagger",
      "q^\\dagger",
      "\\Lambda_c",
      "B_c",
      "R_n(c)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:risk-game",
      "def:evaluator-handle",
      "thm:qstar-uniform-risk-upper"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "At \\(c=c^\\dagger\\), the definition of the contrast-weighted allocation gives\n\\[\nq^\\star=(|1|/2,|-1/4|/2,|-3/4|/2)\n=(1/2,1/8,3/8)=q^\\dagger.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe uniform-risk upper theorem therefore supplies a total estimator\n\\(\\delta_n^\\star\\in[-1,1]\\) under the independent design \\(q^\\dagger\\) and a\nfinite constant \\(B_{c^\\dagger}\\) such that\n\\[\n\\max_{y\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n\\times3}}\n\\mathbb E_y\\!\\left[\n\\{\\delta_n^\\star-\\tau_{c^\\dagger}(y)\\}^2\\right]\n\\le \\frac1n-\\Lambda_{c^\\dagger}n^{-4/3}\n+B_{c^\\dagger}n^{-3/2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(a_j=-1+j/G\\), \\(0\\le j\\le2G\\), be the action grid from the evaluator\ndefinition, and put \\(\\Delta=1/G\\).  Fix a realized observation \\(\\omega\\).\nIf \\(d_\\omega:=\\delta_n^\\star(\\omega)<1\\), choose the unique\n\\(j\\in\\{0,\\ldots,2G-1\\}\\) for which\n\\(a_j\\le d_\\omega<a_{j+1}\\), put\n\\[\n\\lambda_\\omega=\\frac{d_\\omega-a_j}{\\Delta}\\in[0,1),\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nand return \\(a_{j+1}\\) with conditional probability \\(\\lambda_\\omega\\) and\n\\(a_j\\) otherwise.  If \\(d_\\omega=1\\), return \\(a_{2G}=1\\).  Denote the\nresulting randomized action by \\(A_\\omega^{\\mathrm{grid}}\\).  Equation \\((3)\\)\ngives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[A_\\omega^{\\mathrm{grid}}\\mid\\omega]=d_\\omega,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Var}(A_\\omega^{\\mathrm{grid}}\\mid\\omega)\n=\\lambda_\\omega(1-\\lambda_\\omega)\\Delta^2\n\\le\\frac1{4G^2},\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nwhere the variance is zero in the endpoint case.  Thus, for every\n\\(\\tau\\in[-1,1]\\), conditional square completion yields\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E\\!\\left[\n\\{A_\\omega^{\\mathrm{grid}}-\\tau\\}^2\\mid\\omega\\right]\n&=\\{d_\\omega-\\tau\\}^2\n+\\operatorname{Var}(A_\\omega^{\\mathrm{grid}}\\mid\\omega)\\\\\n&\\le\\{d_\\omega-\\tau\\}^2+\\frac1{4G^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nThe probabilities in \\((3)\\) define a feasible Markov kernel for the finite\naction-grid linear program; feasibility does not require them to be rational,\nbecause the program is optimized over real decision variables.  Taking\nexpectations in \\((5)\\), maximizing over the finite schedule set, and then\nusing optimality of \\(U_n\\) and \\((2)\\), gives\n\\[\nU_n\\le \\frac1n-\\Lambda_{c^\\dagger}n^{-4/3}\n+B_{c^\\dagger}n^{-3/2}+\\frac1{4G^2}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nThe independent allocation and the returned grid kernel are an admissible\ndesign--estimator pair in the risk game.  Hence the infimum in that definition\nalso gives\n\\[\nR_n(c^\\dagger)\\le U_n.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIt remains to relate \\((6)\\) to the evaluator's outward error.  Let\n\\(U_{\\Lambda,n}\\) be its rational upper endpoint for\n\\(\\Lambda_{c^\\dagger}\\).  The evaluator may refine the Airy interval and the\nordered-algebraic positive-part enclosure to respective widths at most\n\\(2^{-(n+2)}\\), which is stronger than the required width \\(2^{-n}\\).  Thus it\nmay choose a rational \\(e_{\\mathrm{pr},n}\\) such that\n\\[\n0\\le e_{\\mathrm{pr},n}\n-\\left[n^{4/3}(U_n-n^{-1})+U_{\\Lambda,n}\\right]_+\n\\le2^{-(n+2)},\n\\qquad\n0\\le U_{\\Lambda,n}-\\Lambda_{c^\\dagger}\n\\le2^{-(n+2)}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\nSince \\(G=\\lceil n^{3/4}\\rceil\\ge n^{3/4}\\), equation \\((6)\\) implies\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nn^{4/3}(U_n-n^{-1})+U_{\\Lambda,n}\n&\\le B_{c^\\dagger}n^{-1/6}\n+\\frac{n^{4/3}}{4G^2}\n+U_{\\Lambda,n}-\\Lambda_{c^\\dagger}\\\\\n&\\le (B_{c^\\dagger}+1/4)n^{-1/6}+2^{-(n+2)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\nThe last upper bound is nonnegative.  Combining \\((8)\\)--\\((9)\\) therefore\ngives\n\\[\ne_{\\mathrm{pr},n}\n\\le (B_{c^\\dagger}+1/4)n^{-1/6}+2^{-(n+1)}\n\\le (B_{c^\\dagger}+1/4)n^{-1/6}+2^{-n}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\nBecause \\(B_{c^\\dagger}<\\infty\\), the right side of \\((10)\\) tends to zero,\nwhich proves ordinary convergence of \\(e_{\\mathrm{pr},n}\\).  Neither the\nuniform-risk upper theorem nor any other declared dependency supplies a\ncomputable numerical upper bound for \\(B_{c^\\dagger}\\).  Consequently\n\\((10)\\) alone does not prove a computable modulus of convergence; the\nproposed statement records exactly this limitation.",
    "justification": "Randomized rounding to adjacent grid actions transfers the total independent Airy procedure to the finite rational action program with at most \\(1/(4G^2)\\) additional risk.",
    "gap": "The resulting error converges to zero ordinarily, but the declared existential remainder constant does not provide a computable convergence modulus.",
    "consumer": "It supplies the finite attaining procedure and per-input primal certificate, but not conclusion \\({\\rm(C)}\\) of the open root.",
